  "I32" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: Missing concept definition (S39, S40)."];
  "I43" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: Process efficiency (S55, S56)."];
  "I44" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: Orthogonality/Addressability (S57, S58)."];
  "I6" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: DNA origami is a key tool (S8, S9)."];
  "I7" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: DONs are versatile and accessible (S10, S11)."];
  "I9" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: DONs are attractive for synthetic bio (I7, I8)."];
  "I_Rule_TradeOff" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Rule: If a method increases size but increases cost/effort, it has a drawback."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes the moDON, a reusable DNA-origami monomer design in which a re-routable scaffold enables many interchangeable shape-matching xy interfaces, while single-sequence three-strand z connectors provide rigid, orthogonal, triggerable links, together supporting one-pot high-yield assembly and disassembly of addressable three-dimensional superstructures without relying on many unique components or hierarchical purification steps.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A modular DNA-origami building-block strategy can address the tension between scalable superstructure assembly and retained addressability when it combines reusable monomers, many orthogonal connection sites, and simplified one-step assembly."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) The work reports connection-site modularity through two supported design facts: shape-matching connection sites in the xy direction and a multitude of connection sites achieved by engineering a re-routable scaffold."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) DNA origami nanostructures can be easily designed and synthesized."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) A large variety of DNA origami nanostructures have been published."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) A variety of different strategies have been reported to increase DON sizes."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Strategies include lambda/M13 hybrid, orthogonal scaffolds, unscaffolded structures, slats, or hierarchical assembly."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Both approaches have bottlenecks: increased effort for scaffold production and unique staples."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) The problem of individually designed staples affects unscaffolded nanostructures employing SST assembly."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Using 10,000 unique ssDNA tiles, superstructures in the Gigadalton scale have been constructed."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) The largest superstructures reported were constructed using DNA origami slats."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Structures using DNA origami slats reached several µm and GDa in size and are cost effective."];
  "S26" [label="(26,0,0) Another approach is the construction of superstructures with ssDNA connectors."];
  "S27" [label="(27,0,0) ssDNA connector construction requires many unique connectors, making it susceptible to unwanted interactions."];
  "S28" [label="(28,0,0) GDa sizes can be reached through hierarchical assembly."];
  "S29" [label="(29,0,0) Hierarchical assembly has led to structures measuring 0.5 µm (2D) and 3D structures up to GDa scale."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Subunits may be reusable for different structures or systems."];
  "S30" [label="(30,0,0) High structural control and potential size make hierarchical assembly an excellent approach."];
  "S31" [label="(31,0,0) Design and construction of hierarchical assembly structures are faced with different challenges."];
  "S32" [label="(32,0,0) Assemblies of self-complementary DONs lead to repetitive, homomultimeric superstructures."];
  "S33" [label="(33,0,0) The approach of using self-complementary DNA origami nanostructures is simple and cost-effective."];
  "S34" [label="(34,0,0) Self-complementary approach results in loss of structural control beyond the repetitive subunit."];
  "S35" [label="(35,0,0) It is impossible to controllably retain site-specific addressability beyond the single monomer with self-complementary DONs."];
  "S36" [label="(36,0,0) Assemblies made up of many different DONs yield finite, heteromultimeric superstructures."];
  "S37" [label="(37,0,0) With many different DONs, structural control is high and site-specific addressability is retained."];
  "S39" [label="(39,0,0) Currently, concepts combining both low cost and high structural versatility are missing."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Modularity allows for low-effort, cost-effective assembly."];
  "S40" [label="(40,0,0) Such concepts could alleviate issues and allow controlled assembly with retained addressability."];
  "S45" [label="(45,0,0) The first method of modularity was created for shape-matching connection sites in the xy-direction."];
  "S46" [label="(46,0,0) A multitude of connection sites was achieved by engineering a re-routable scaffold."];
  "S55" [label="(55,0,0) Superstructures could be assembled in one-step reactions from monomers."];
  "S56" [label="(56,0,0) The one-step assembly of superstructures greatly simplifies construction and increases yield."];
  "S57" [label="(57,0,0) Xy- and z-connections are fully orthogonal allowing for parallel and selective assembly."];
  "S58" [label="(58,0,0) Site-specific addressability of each monomer is fully retained."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Structural DNA nanotechnology is a widely used construction approach."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) DNA origami is an indispensable tool for nanoscale engineering."];
